---
.title = "Codegen",
.description = "Print a tree back to JavaScript or TypeScript source, with type stripping, minification, and source maps.",
.author = "Arshad Yaseen",
.date = @date("2026-06-23T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
---
The codegen prints a tree back to source in one walk, so its output is always valid and faithful to the tree.

## [JavaScript]($section.id('javascript'))

```bash
npm install yuku-codegen
```

```js
import { generate } from "yuku-codegen";
import { parse } from "yuku-parser";

const { code, map, diagnostics } = generate(parse("const x = 1 + 2;").program);
```

The same printer in plain JavaScript, for any ESTree AST, with [byte-identical output](/testing/#codegen), source maps included. It runs 2.6× faster than `@babel/generator`, or 3× with source maps on. See [yuku-codegen on npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/yuku-codegen) for the full API.

## [Zig]($section.id('zig'))

```zig
var tree = try parser.parse(allocator, "const x: number = 1;", .{ .lang = .ts });
defer tree.deinit();

const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{ .strip = true });
defer result.deinit(allocator);

std.debug.print("{s}\n", .{result.code}); // const x = 1;
```

`generate` leaves the tree unchanged and returns the `code`, its `diagnostics` as `ast.Diagnostic`, and the source `map`, owned by the allocator it was given and freed with `result.deinit`. It fails with `error.OutOfMemory` or `error.InvalidJSXFactory` for an invalid factory name.

## [Options]($section.id('options'))

| Field        | Default     | Description                                                                                                                                         |
| ------------ | ----------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `jsx`        | `null`      | Preserve JSX or lower it with classic factories, see [JSX](#jsx)                                                                                    |
| `strip`      | `false`     | Drop TypeScript-only syntax, see [Type stripping](#type-stripping)                                                                                  |
| `minify`     | `false`     | Size-reducing syntax rewrites, see [Minification](#minification)                                                                                    |
| `format`     | `.pretty`   | `.compact` emits only the whitespace the grammar requires                                                                                           |
| `indent`     | `2`         | Spaces per level                                                                                                                                    |
| `quotes`     | `.preserve` | `.preserve`, `.double`, `.single`, or `.shortest`                                                                                                   |
| `comments`   | `.some`     | Which [attached comments](/parser/ast/#comments) to print, `.some` for legal headers, JSDoc, and annotations, or `.all`, `.none`, `.line`, `.block` |
| `source_map` | `null`      | Emit a Source Map V3, see [Source maps](#source-maps)                                                                                               |

Every option is independent, so they compose freely. Comments print from the nodes they are attached to, so parse with `.comments = .attached` or `.both` to keep them.

## [JSX]($section.id('jsx'))

`.jsx = .{}` lowers JSX while printing with the classic `React.createElement` and `React.Fragment`
factories. Configure identifiers or dotted names already in scope to use another runtime.

```zig
const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{
    .jsx = .{ .pragma = "h", .pragma_frag = "Fragment" },
    .strip = true,
});
```

The JavaScript API accepts `{ jsx: true }` or `{ jsx: { pragma: "h", pragmaFrag: "Fragment" } }`.
JSX is preserved when the option is unset, `null` in Zig or `"preserve"` in JavaScript, so
`{ jsx: "preserve", strip: true }` retains JSX for a framework transform such as Vue's JSX
plugin. A custom factory alone does not implement Vue directives or slots.

JSX tag type arguments are erased. Text follows JSX whitespace normalization, and text and quoted
attributes decode XHTML entities. Spreads retain evaluation order. Attached comments follow
`comments`, including those on erased types, closing names, and closing fragments. The runtime
is classic. Imports and comment pragmas are not generated. Calls to the default React factories
carry `/* @__PURE__ */` annotations so bundlers can drop unused elements; custom factories are
annotated only with `pure = true`, since the annotation claims the call has no effects.

## [Type stripping]($section.id('type-stripping'))

`strip` prints TypeScript as JavaScript in the same walk, skipping the nodes and fields the parser already marked as TypeScript. Types, interfaces, generics, assertions, `satisfies`, and `declare` strip cleanly. Features that emit runtime code, such as `enum`, `namespace`, and parameter properties, are reported in `diagnostics` and left out, since converting them is transpilation, not stripping.

## [Minification]($section.id('minification'))

`minify` rewrites the syntax, and combined with `.compact` and `.shortest` it is full minification.

```zig
const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{
    .minify = true,
    .format = .compact,
    .quotes = .shortest,
});
// const enabled = true; const size = 1000000;
// prints as const enabled=!0;const size=1e6
```

- `true` and `false` become `!0` and `!1`.
- Numbers take their shortest form, so `1000000` becomes `1e6` and `0.5` becomes `.5`.
- `obj["foo"]` becomes `obj.foo`, and `{ "foo": x }` becomes `{ foo: x }`.
- `</script`, `<!--`, and `-->` are escaped, so the output is safe inside a `<script>` tag.

## [Source maps]($section.id('source-maps'))

```zig
const result = try parser.codegen.generate(allocator, &tree, .{
    .source_map = .{
        .source = source,           // required for a map
        .file = "out.js",
        .source_file_name = "in.js",
        .source_root = null,
        .sources_content = source,
    },
});
defer result.deinit(allocator);

const map = result.map.?; // version, file, source_root, sources, sources_content, names, mappings
```

Mappings come from the same walk that prints the code, with columns in UTF-16 code units, the convention of browser devtools and source map libraries.

A build that never needs maps can compile them out, for a smaller binary. `map` is then always `null`.

```zig
const yuku = b.dependency("yuku", .{ .@"codegen-source-maps" = false });
```
